00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:32.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-And hearing that this is

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:35.161 align:start size:94% position:6%
a public-access show.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, I love the rhythm of it
-Thank you, thank you.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:41.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
We do love it, too.
So this is a wonderful fil

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:44.137 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the 1969
Harlem Cultural Festival.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:45.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is not something
I knew about.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:47.273 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
-And --

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:47.807 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Me neither.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:49.442 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's the shocking thing
right?

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:51.911 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, you have
all this knowledge of,

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:56.516
especially music history, an
when you first got word of this,

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:57.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
you also
didn't think it was real.

00:01:57.884 --> 00:02:00.353 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I thought I had
all the knowledge in the world

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:02.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, 'cause,
you know, I was --

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:06.192
There was a period where, like
I was the guy -- I was YouTube

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:07.460 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was the guy
that everyone called on

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.599 size:88% position:44%
for any sort of archive.
So, naturally, when I --

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:14.534 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, when they requeste
a meeting with me,

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:17.237
you know, I googled and I called
a few music experts,

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:19.539 align:start size:94% position:6%
and none of them
ever heard of this thing.

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:20.840 size:81% position:41% line:92%
So I was very, very --

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.775 align:start size:94% position:6%
They hate when I say this,
but I -- you know,

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:23.743 line:92%
I thought they just -- like,

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:24.978 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Why don't they just ask
for 'Fallon' tickets?

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, it's easier."
-These were -- these were --

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:29.048 align:start size:94% position:6%
When you say this, these are
guys who are calling you,

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:31.584 align:start size:94% position:6%
saying, "We have this footage,
and we want you to see it.

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:33.586 align:start size:94% position:6%
-My two producers,
David Dinerstein

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:36.990 size:84% position:42%
and Robert Fyvolent,
they said that they had

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
over 40 hours
of this mythical Stevie Wonder

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:43.196
Sly and the Family Stone concert
that happened in Harlem.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:46.533 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
after like nine people told me

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:47.233 size:81% position:41% line:92%
"I never heard of it,"

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:49.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they were like of age,
at the time,

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:51.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just, like, "Nah.
There's no way this happened."

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:55.241
-And so, I mean, it must hav
been, for someone like you

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:57.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
and for the kind of artist
that are in this film,

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.445 size:84% position:42%
and what was it like
when you first sat down

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:00.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
and started watching
this footage?

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.116 line:92%
Was it like the Holy Grail

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:04.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's --
It's past the Holy Grail.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, this is what they call
lightning in a bottle.

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:11.891
And then, like, all my arrogance
sort of dissipated,

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:15.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
and suddenly, like,
I -- I became worried.

00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:18.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, why would they even
trust me to tell this story?

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:19.966 line:92%
Like, a first-time storyteller

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:23.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, yeah.
I'll -- I'll just say that

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:28.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
I realized that I had, like,
the most valuable --

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:30.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm sorry.
Am I giving my bracket awa

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:33.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
if I say the word "vase"?
-No, go for it.

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:34.914 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm sorry.
"Vayse."

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:40.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I knew I had an
important artifact in my hand.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.955 line:92%
So, the first thing I did was,

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:45.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, I assembled
a crack team of experts.

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Mm-hmm.
-Shout-out to my producer,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.664 align:start size:94% position:6%
Joseph Patel,
and my editor Josh, you know

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.933
And, you know, everyone else
involved with the project.

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:58.871
But, um, I -- I just wante
to do it because, you know

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:01.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm an obsessive
for history, and so...

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:02.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
And it's interesting.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:05.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, it seemed like
it was not just lost,

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:06.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
in that people
didn't know what happened,

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:09.182
but it seemed like
some of the people you talked to

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:10.016 line:92%
that even performed at it,

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:12.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
almost, to some degree,
had forgotten.

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:13.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Cause we have this great thin
in the film,

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is you show -- we get to
watch people in real-time

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:18.324 line:92%
watch their old performances

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:21.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Very meta. Yeah.
It's -- It's sort of that

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:25.565
"Harry Met Sally" situatio
where you get to reflect

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:27.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
and watch people
watch themselves.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
I thought it was important
only because,

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:34.307
just in my research, I had s
many goose-bump moments of like,

00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:36.476 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Wow, wow, wow!",
that sort of thing,

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:39.145 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I wanted them to have
the same response, too.

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:43.349
I wanted to catch their -- their
reaction when they first saw it.

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:44.817 line:92%
And, yeah, it was complete -

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:48.655 size:81% position:41%
Like, magic constantly
jumps from the screen

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:49.956 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you watch
these performances.

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:52.925 size:88% position:44%
-And another magic thing
that's happened for you

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:54.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that some people
have reached out and said

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
they not only have seen
themselves in the footage,

00:04:56.596 --> 00:05:00.066 size:88% position:44%
they've seen, you know,
departed family members.

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:02.568
-Yeah. That --
That, to me, is my favorite part

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:03.303 size:81% position:41% line:92%
of this whole process.

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:08.474
Um, all throughout social media,
like, every day, there's a -

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:13.446
a guy who hasn't seen
or has any photos of his brother

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:15.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
who passed away
when he was like 20,

00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:19.919
like, during like 1970, '71,
and we have a close-up of them

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:22.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, like, the whole family
is like, you know,

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:26.893
they get their opportunity t
see their uncle as a teenager.

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:30.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a woman that discovere
her great-grandmother

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:34.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the front row,
screaming at Stevie Wonder

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:36.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, and daily,
I get things of,

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:37.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, that's me when I was 14!"
You know?

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:40.073
So that's -- that's what's
happening right now. Yeah.

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:42.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Um, you had actually seen
footage of this before,

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:43.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you didn't know
what it was for.

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:45.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
You were in Japan.
-Yeah.

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:47.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And where did you see it?
-Okay.

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:51.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, when The Roots
first went to Japan, in 1997

00:05:51.718 --> 00:05:53.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
back when things
were, like, more localized

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:57.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the Internet didn't connec
the world at the same time

00:05:57.757 --> 00:06:02.395
um, if you wanted to see
anything from my childhood

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:07.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
old games, toys, artifacts
afro pics, whatever,

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
you went to Tokyo.
-Really?

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:11.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Because, I mean,
they have such an affinity

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:13.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
for, like,
'70s culture over there.

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:19.078
And so, my translator looked
at my afro and was like --

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:21.481
She kind of put two and tw
together like, "You know,

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:24.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
you remind me of Don Cornelius
from 'Soul Train,'"

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.151 line:92%
and so, I was like, "Okay.

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:29.222
And then, she took me to a place
called the Soul Train Café

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:32.325 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is basically
kind of like a Friday's...

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:34.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
...with better fonts.

00:06:34.694 --> 00:06:37.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And there was, like, monitors

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.333
all over the restaurant,
and I just happened to see

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:45.138
maybe a two- or three-minute
clip of Sly and the Family Stone

00:06:45.138 --> 00:06:46.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
from one of
the far-away cameras.

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:50.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, at the time,
I didn't realize I was watchin

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:51.177 line:92%
the Harlem Cultural Festival

00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:53.579 size:81% position:41%
I thought it was like
Montreux Jazz Festival

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:54.647 line:92%
or something, like, in Europe.

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:57.784
-And you -- I want to refer back
to something you said

00:06:57.784 --> 00:06:58.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
about how
you used to be YouTube.

00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:02.822 align:start size:94% position:6%
You took this very seriously
You carried around, like,

00:07:02.822 --> 00:07:06.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
bags full of tapes, becaus
people had this expectation...

00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:07.894
-Yeah.
-...that even when you traveled,

00:07:07.894 --> 00:07:10.430
they could ask you
for this sort of piece of arcana

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:13.566
that no one else could get
-So I had two Kipling bags

00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:15.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
probably the size
of this couch right here.

00:07:15.568 --> 00:07:18.538 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-huh.
-And then, once I went

00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:20.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the Soul Train Café --
you know, again,

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:22.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
this is before the Internet,
before you can

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:25.711
instantly look at -- back at
something from your childhood.

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:27.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
All I have
are memories at that point

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:29.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so,
I asked this woman, like,

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:32.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Where can I find,
like, these clips?

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:34.353
You talk about 'Soul Train.'
I haven't seen that in 25 years.

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:38.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, where is it?"
And, um, she took me to this

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:41.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
magical, like,
Video City in Tokyo.

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:43.329 line:92%
And I stayed an extra two days

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:48.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, I probably purchased
easily like $15,000 worth of -

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's vase --
That's vase money.

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:52.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, vase money.
Yeah, getting it back into

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:54.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
the States
was a little bit weird.

00:07:54.207 --> 00:07:57.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
But every --
from 1997 to about 2004,

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.145 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you saw me
in the streets of New York

00:07:59.145 --> 00:07:59.946 line:92%
I didn't have a backpack on.

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:03.616
I had two giant Kipling case
that I took with me everywhere

00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:06.652 align:start size:94% position:6%
And this is what --
Like, when I worked on, like

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:08.588 size:75% position:38%
Erykah Badu's record
or D'Angelo's record

00:08:08.588 --> 00:08:11.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
or Common's album
or any artist I worked with,

00:08:11.624 --> 00:08:13.960 size:78% position:39%
we'd spend five hours
just going through

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.795 line:92%
what we call, like, the treats

00:08:15.795 --> 00:08:18.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's how we made,
like, the records

00:08:18.164 --> 00:08:20.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I'm sort of
associated with and known for.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:21.968 line:92%
Like, it's just based on that.

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:24.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then, YouTube came
and put me out of business

00:08:24.337 --> 00:08:25.104 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
So...

00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:27.940
-You -- You also mentioned
